                <pb facs="0209" n="197" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg197"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/2892: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-05-13T13:00:00+02:00">May 13, 1940—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 13—11:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1322. As far as the Embassy is aware none of the Berlin papers have as yet
                    carried any mention of the exchange of messages between President Roosevelt and
                    the King of the Belgians. The DNB ticker service, however, issued yesterday
                    evening under a Brussels dateline the following report reading as follows in
                    translation: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“King Leopold addressed a message to President Roosevelt, in which he
                            requested Roosevelt to support Belgium with his full authority. In his
                            reply President Roosevelt expressed the hope that German policy would be
                            wrecked.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
